\begin_layout Section
Analogy with Classical Mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Newton's Second Law
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A law of physics can be understood as a rule that describes—
usually by means of a mathematical formulation—
how certain physical systems behave
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Siddiqui2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Newton's second law,
 for example,
 can be expressed by the equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F\left(x,t\right)=m\frac{d^{2}}{dt^{2}}x\left(t\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the position of a particle of mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 relative to the origin at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 When we solve this differential equation under certain initial conditions,
 we obtain as a solution a function 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 once the forces acting on the particle and its initial conditions are specified,
 Newton's second law determines its temporal evolution:
 solving the equation provides us with the equation of motion of the particle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 for a free particle(
\begin_inset Formula $F=0$
\end_inset

),
 we can obtain its equation of motion from Newton's second law:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0=m\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining the velocity as 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)\equiv\frac{dx}{dt}$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
0 & =\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}\\
0 & =\frac{dv}{dt}\\
0\cdot\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt' & =\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dv}{dt'}dt'\\
0 & =v\left(t\right)-v\left(t_{0}\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)=v\left(t_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 the particle's velocity is constant.
 Denoting this constant velocity by 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
v\left(t\right) & =v\left(t_{0}\right)\\
\frac{dx}{dt} & =v\\
\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dx}{dt'}dt' & =v\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt'\\
x\left(t\right)-x\left(t_{0}\right) & =v\left(t-t_{0}\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t_{0}\right)=x_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{0}=0$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x\left(t\right)=x_{0}+vt
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is easily interpretable as the position of the particle at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Schrödinger Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The quantum mechanical analogue of Newton's second law is the Schrödinger equation.
 In one dimension and in the non-relativistic case,
 the Schrödinger equation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi\left(x,t\right)=-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\Psi\left(x,t\right)+V\left(x,t\right)\Psi\left(x,t\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corresponding equation for a free particle (
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(x,t\right)=0$
\end_inset

) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}\Psi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Using the method of separation of variables,
 that is,
 assuming that the solution can be separated into a spatial part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and a temporal part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right) & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right)\\
i\hbar\frac{1}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since one side depends only on the spatial dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

) and the other only on the temporal dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

),
 the only way this can be true is if both sides are equal to a constant:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

.
 Solving then for the temporal part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{i\hbar}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =E\\
\frac{d\Pi}{dt} & =-\frac{iE}{\hbar}\Pi
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the derivative of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is equal to the function itself multiplied by a constant,
 we have an exponential solution 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For the spatial part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\Phi
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since this is a second-order linear homogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients,
 we can look for solutions of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)=e^{\pm ikx}$
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{ikx}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-ikx}$
\end_inset

 are two linearly independent solutions to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d^{2}\Phi}{dx^{2}}=-k^{2}\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 By the linearity of the equation,
 its general solution is a linear combination of these solutions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Pi\left(t\right)\Phi\left(x\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)\left(B\exp\left(-i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)+C\exp\left(i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Or,
 writing 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}=2mE/\hbar^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=\frac{E}{\hbar}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\Psi\left(x,t\right) & =K_{1}e^{-i\left(kx+\omega t\right)}+K_{2}e^{i\left(kx-\omega t\right)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}$
\end_inset

 are constants determined by the conditions imposed on the solution.
 Here,
 some questions already arise.
 When we solved Newton's second law,
 we obtained 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which described the position of the particle.
 When we solve the Schrödinger equation,
 we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 but what is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

?
 What does 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 describe?
\end_layout

